NATIVELY: Here's the schema I'd go with, keeping the read-heavy access pattern in mind.

```sql
CREATE TABLE users (
    id          BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    handle      TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,
    display_name TEXT NOT NULL,
    created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE TABLE posts (
    id          BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    author_id   BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id),
    body        TEXT NOT NULL,
    created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

CREATE TABLE likes (
    user_id     BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id),
    post_id     BIGINT NOT NULL REFERENCES posts(id),
    created_at  TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, post_id)
);

CREATE INDEX idx_posts_author_created ON posts (author_id, created_at DESC);
CREATE INDEX idx_posts_created        ON posts (created_at DESC);
CREATE INDEX idx_likes_post           ON likes (post_id);
```

Three tables: users, posts, and a likes join table. The composite primary key on `(user_id, post_id)` enforces one like per user per post and gives you the "has this user liked this post" lookup for free. I index `likes(post_id)` so counting likes on a post is a cheap index scan, and index posts by `created_at DESC` for the feed.

Since it's read-heavy, I'd keep a denormalized `like_count` on posts, updated on write, so the feed doesn't aggregate the likes table on every scroll. The tradeoff is a counter that can drift, so I'd reconcile it periodically or make the increment part of the same transaction as the like insert.

